A||||5||82-T/A||Rust in the trunk!!!!!!!||||8||
Z||000000||82-T/A||11-24-2000||05:09 PM||||Well, I'm probably going to start restoring my 84 Fiero 2m4 SE this weekend. My parents house is finished and they're letting me use their garage.<P>One of the first things I noticed was that there is rust in the trunk area. On both sides. Not at the bottom, but on the upper level of the trunk area (where the golf bag goes). There are two huge holes... and the metal is flaking off. It looks old. Since I live in Florida, I don't think it's spreading. The car originally came from North Dakota so thats probably the reason. Anyway,<P>whats the best way to patch up these holes? I have no experience doing this, but I want to attempt it myself. I've gotten rid of rust before on my 82 TransAm that I used to own.. but that was a long time ago..<P>anyone that has any hints or ideas... feel free to shoot..<P><BR>Thanks!<P>Todd<P>------------------<BR>Todd Jaspers<BR>1997 Pontiac GrandAm SE<BR>1987 Pontiac Fiero SE/V6<BR>1984 Pontiac Fiero 2m4 SE||||reg||8
Z||000001||StuGood||11-24-2000||06:50 PM||||If you really hate rust, I suppose you could fiberglass it.  The polyester resin type is  normally runny and difficult to use on vertical surfaces.  But for this, you don't have to worry so much about the surface finish. The trunk carpet will cover it up, and of course the outside of the trunk box doesn't show, either.<P>Or could rivet in some sheet metal, but then you'd end up fiberglassing anyway to seal it up.  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Seanh||11-24-2000||07:09 PM||||I knocked out all the rust, and sanded the edges. Then sprayed with rust-converter and painted it black. You then could (but I didnt) rivit sheet metal over it and seal the edges up.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Fformula88||11-25-2000||12:16 AM||||I may have the answer for runny fiberglass!  I recently got a fiberglass repair kit.  The stuff is like Bondo, but fiberglass.  So it isn't runny like the normal resins and stuff.  I got it at pep boys. and used it to fix a cracked front fender.  If the holes aren't very big, you may want to consider it.  It comes with a piece of fiberglass cloth which you smear the stuff on... the put it over whatever your patching.  <P>It won't suck up water... but whatever you do, DO NOT USE BONDO.  The moisture will soak into it and your repair won't last long.  <P>This problem seems to be a design defect.  My 88 Formula also has a lot of rust in the same spot, and has never seen a winter.  Moisture gets up in there and just sits.    ||||reg||1
Z||000004||82-T/A||11-25-2000||09:43 AM||||Ok, thanks guys... I'll use all these ideas!!<P>I will be starting on the car this Tuesday when I drive it over to my parents house.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Todd Jaspers<BR>1997 Pontiac GrandAm SE<BR>1987 Pontiac Fiero SE/V6<BR>1984 Pontiac Fiero 2m4 SE||||reg||1
Z||000005||deceler8||11-25-2000||10:13 AM||||Once you do get it fixed, make sure your rear wheel liners are complete and not cracked or torn. <P>You might also think about getting some mud flaps...the right sized set actually looks OK on a Fiero...||||reg||1
